Trained alone, the robots with plenty of data do well, and the one with 3 episodes reaches the goal in 60% of held-out tasks (@fig-pooling). Trained together, with a separate output head per robot, four motors rises to 80% and three wheels from 84% to 91%, while the two data-rich robots lose nothing. The shared layers learn how to get round the post from everyone's demos; each head only has to learn its robot's motor mapping, which is far less to learn from 3 episodes. That is positive transfer. It is not guaranteed: four motors gained in 7 of the 8 seeds, not all of them.


