### Feeding the model: batches and buffers {#pipeline-intuition}

Pooled data break the simple assumption that every sample has the same shape. One data set has a single third-person camera, another adds two wrist cameras [▶ 39:03](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=2343). **Padding** every sample to the longest one is easy, but in a mixed batch much of the compute goes on zeros [▶ 40:05](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=2405). **Sequence packing** lays the samples end to end instead, so every token is real; the price is bookkeeping: the start of each sample, an attention mask that keeps samples from looking at each other, and positions that restart at each sample [▶ 40:35](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=2435) [▶ 41:39](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=2499). In the page's example batch, two samples with 1 camera and two with 3, padding computes 232 tokens for 168 real ones, 28% wasted ([worked example](#ex-packing)).

How randomly the loader mixes data mattered as much. Octo streamed trajectories from disk into a **shuffle buffer** and drew batches from it; with a buffer of 20,000 frames, batches held frames from too few trajectories and the policy generalized poorly, and enlarging it to 500,000 made a large difference with nothing else changed [▶ 42:41](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=2561) [▶ 43:43](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=2623). The toy streams 5\,000 trajectories of 100 frames and draws batches of 256.

::: {#cell-fig-buffer .cell execution_count=6}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Third toy: the average number of different trajectories in a batch of 256 frames, against the shuffle buffer's size (5,000 trajectories of 100 frames, streamed one trajectory at a time; 30 batches each). The dashed line is what drawing frames uniformly from the whole data set would give.](w09-generalist-robot-policies_files/figure-html/fig-buffer-output-1.png){#fig-buffer width=552 height=283 fig-alt='Line chart with a log x-axis for buffer size: 2,000, 20,000, 100,000 and 500,000 frames. The y-axis is distinct trajectories per batch, 0 to about 260. The line rises from about 48 at 2,000 to about 156 at 20,000, about 226 at 100,000 and about 249 at 500,000, where it meets a dashed horizontal line at about 250 labelled uniform draws.'}
:::
:::


A batch from a 2\,000-frame buffer holds frames from only 48 trajectories on average, so each gradient step sees a few situations many times over; a 20\,000-frame buffer gives 156, and only a buffer near the size of the data set approaches the 250 of truly random draws (@fig-buffer). Loading by random reads from a flat index avoids the problem at the price of fast storage [▶ 44:14](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=2654).

### Learning from data of mixed quality {#quality-intuition}

Pooling also mixes skill levels: careful demos, hesitant ones, the robot's own failed attempts. Cloning all of it copies the average, mistakes included. This week's guest showed that adding the lowest-quality data *hurt* π0.7 when the model was not told about quality, and *helped* when each episode came with metadata on its speed and quality [▶ 10:33](https://youtu.be/pzolgvyWEFY?t=633). The discussion paper π\*0.6 does this per action: a value function estimates each action's advantage, the policy is trained on all data with one more input saying whether the advantage beat a threshold, and at test time it is asked for the good actions. Unlike filtering, nothing is thrown away: the bad steps still teach what the situations look like. The [corridor example](#ex-advantage) works this out exactly.

### Evaluate in simulation, for rankings {#eval-intuition}

A generalist must be tested on many tasks and robots, and real evaluation is slow and fragile: someone bumps a camera, a motor is replaced and the joint readings shift, and a single trial takes minutes [▶ 54:10](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=3250) [▶ 55:13](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=3313). Simulation is cheaper and reproducible [▶ 56:47](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=3407). SIMPLER evaluates policies trained only on real data in simulated copies of the real setups [▶ 57:18](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=3438), after narrowing two gaps: the **control gap**, by system identification so that the simulated arm responds to commands like the real one [▶ 58:52](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=3532), and the **visual gap**, by green-screening real backgrounds behind simulated objects and projecting real textures onto them [▶ 59:23](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=3563). What it promises is a ranking, not absolute numbers: 50% in simulation does not mean 50% on the robot, but if policy A beats B in SIMPLER, it should on the real robot too [▶ 60:55](https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo?t=3655).

The second toy tests the control gap only. Nine policies, from 3 cloning budgets each run at 3 speed factors, are scored on the "real" point robot, whose motors are noisy, and in simulators whose motor gain is wrong (0.4 instead of 1) or estimated by system identification from 100 test commands.

::: {#cell-fig-simreal .cell execution_count=7}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Second toy (policy family 0): each dot is one of 9 policies, its real success rate (x) against its success rate in a simulator (y). Left: uncalibrated, motor gain 0.4 and no noise. Middle: gain identified from 100 real commands, no noise. Right: gain and motor noise identified. The diagonal marks equal success.](w09-generalist-robot-policies_files/figure-html/fig-simreal-output-1.png){#fig-simreal width=659 height=265 fig-alt='Three square scatter plots side by side, each with 9 dots and a dashed diagonal, both axes from 0 to 1. Left, uncalibrated: three dots sit at 0 in simulation although their real success is between about 0.65 and 0.8; the other six lie close to the diagonal. Middle, gain only: all dots lie on or above the diagonal, the three slowest policies furthest above it, by about 0.1 to 0.16. Right, gain and noise: all nine dots lie on the diagonal.'}
:::
